/** @page python_importguide Importing Python APIs into Qore

    @tableofcontents

    To import %Python APIs and definitions into a %Qore program, the following python-module-specific module
    command can be used, which also causes the python module to be loaded and initialized if necessary:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(python) import module[.symbol|*] @endcode imports the given %Python module into %Python
      and then imports the module or optionally only the given symbol into the %Qore program from %Python.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%module-cmd(python) import math.sin

auto sub get(auto arg) {
    return math::sin(arg);
}
    @endcode

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE);
p.loadModule("python");
p.issueModuleCmd("python", "import math");
    @endcode

    %Python functions become %Qore functions, %Python classes %Qore classes, and other attributes of the module %Qore
    constants with their converted values (see @ref python_typesguide).  The builtin functions and classes of %Python
    (the \c builtins module) are available in the \c Python::builtins namespace of every %Program that loads the
    module (ex: \c Python::builtins::len()); the constants of the \c builtins module are not imported, as they are
    %Python objects (ex: \c exit and \c quit, which would hide %Qore's @ref Qore::exit() "exit()") or values with
    %Qore equivalents (\c True, \c False, \c None).

    If the module defines \c __all__ (a list or another sequence of names), only the names in it are imported.

    The methods of a %Python class whose names have a special meaning in %Qore classes (\c constructor,
    \c destructor, \c copy, \c methodGate, \c memberGate and \c memberNotification) are imported with a \c _
    prefix: ex: the \c copy() method of a %Python class is \c _copy() in %Qore.

    @section python_classes_in_qore Python Classes in Qore

    The methods of a %Python class are methods of its %Qore class, and its static methods and class methods
    (\c \@staticmethod, \c \@classmethod) are static methods; a class method gets the class it is called for: the
    class methods that a class inherits are static methods of its %Qore class that get the class, not the base class
    that defines them.  All the base classes of a %Python class are parent classes of its %Qore class, in the order of
    their declaration.

    The attributes of a %Python object are read and set as members of the %Qore object (ex: \c obj.attr): assigning
    a member sets the attribute, and \c remove and \c delete delete it (\c remove returns its previous value), as
    in %Python, so properties, \c __slots__ and \c __setattr__() apply; this also applies to %Qore subclasses of
    %Python classes.  An assignment that changes the value of the attribute (ex: <tt>obj.count += 1</tt>,
    <tt>obj.h.key = value</tt>) reads the attribute, changes a %Qore copy of it and assigns the result: a changed
    \c dict or \c list is a new %Python object, and the sequence is not atomic.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
PythonProgram p("class Account:
    def __init__(self):
        self.balance = 0
    def report(self):
        return f'{self.owner}: {self.balance}'
", "account.py");
object account = p.callFunction("Account");
account.balance += 100;
account.owner = "sales";
# "sales: 100"
printf("%s\n", account.report());
    @endcode

    Other methods of
    the object are called as %Python gets them from the object (with \c getattr()): callable attributes of the
    object (ex: a \c lambda or a \c functools.partial object stored in the object) and methods added to its class
    after its %Qore class was created are called with all the arguments; calling an attribute that is not callable
    raises a \c METHOD-DOES-NOT-EXIST exception.

    A %Python iterator (an object with a \c __next__() method, ex: a generator) is an
    @ref Qore::AbstractIterator "AbstractIterator" in %Qore, so it can be iterated with \c foreach and the other
    iterator APIs (unless its class has attributes named \c next, \c getValue or \c valid):

    @code{.py}
PythonProgram p("def squares(n):\n    for i in range(n):\n        yield i * i\n", "gen.py");
# prints 0 1 4
foreach int v in (p.callFunction("squares", 3)) {
    printf("%d\n", v);
}
    @endcode

    A coroutine can be run with \c asyncio.run() in a %Python function called from %Qore.

    @subsection python_qore_overrides Qore Classes Inheriting Python Classes

    When a %Qore class inherits a %Python class and overrides its methods, %Python code calls the %Qore overrides:
    the %Python object of a %Qore object of such a class is an object of a %Python subclass of the %Python class,
    created for the %Qore class, whose methods that the %Qore class overrides (with public normal methods) call the
    %Qore methods.  The overrides are called also from the \c __init__() method of the %Python class, and a %Qore
    override can call the %Python method with the name of the parent class (ex: \c Base::hook()).  When the %Python
    object is returned to %Qore, it is the %Qore object.  A %Qore exception raised by an override is raised in the
    %Python code that called it (see @ref python_exceptionsguide).

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires python

Program pgm(PO_NEW_STYLE);
pgm.issueModuleCmd("python", "parse report.py #
class Report:
    def render(self):
        return '[' + self.title() + ']'
    def title(self):
        return 'report'
");
pgm.issueModuleCmd("python", "export-class Report");
pgm.parse("
class SalesReport inherits Report {
    string title() {
        return 'sales';
    }
}
string sub render() {
    return (new SalesReport()).render();
}
", "sales");

# "[sales]": Report.render() in Python calls the Qore override
printf("%s\n", pgm.callFunction("render"));
    @endcode

    @note The %Python object of such a %Qore object holds a weak reference to it: calling an override after the
    %Qore object was deleted raises an \c OBJECT-ALREADY-DELETED exception.  Before %Python 3.15, a %Python class whose
    objects cannot have an attribute slot (ex: a subclass of \c tuple) cannot be inherited by a %Qore class that
    overrides its methods.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires python

PythonProgram p("
class Order:
    def __init__(self):
        self.total = lambda tax: 100 * (1 + tax)
    @classmethod
    def create(cls):
        return cls()

class RushOrder(Order):
    pass

def new_order():
    return RushOrder.create()
", "orders.py");

object order = p.callFunction("new_order");
# "RushOrder": the class method gets the subclass
printf("%s\n", order.create().className());
# 120.0: a callable attribute of the object
printf("%y\n", order.total(0.2));
    @endcode

    @section python_module_path Set the Python Module Path

    Elements can be added to the %Python import module path by using the following module command:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(python) add-module-path <path>@endcode

    The path given is also subjected to environment variable substitutions; the environment variable name must be
    prefixed with a \c "$" char and then can be given either surrounded by \c "()" or \c "{}" brackets, of if no
    brackets are used, the environment variable must fit the following pattern: [a-zA-Z0-9_] (alphanumeric + \c "_").

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%module-cmd(python) add-module-path ${MY_PYTHON_MODS}
%module-cmd(python) import my-python-mod
    @endcode

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE);
p.loadModule("python");
p.issueModuleCmd("python", "add-module-path " + get_script_dir());
p.issueModuleCmd("python", "import my-python-module");
    @endcode

    @section python_immediate_parsing Parsing Python Code Immediately

    %Python code can be parsed immediately using the following module command:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(python) parse <filename> <Python source>@endcode

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE);
p.loadModule("python");
string src = "
class PyTest:
    def get(self):
        return 3

def py_test():
    return 'from python'
";
p.issueModuleCmd("python", "parse pytest.py " + src);
    @endcode

    @section python_class_export Exporting Python Classes to Qore

    %Python classes can be explicitly exported to %Qore with the following module command:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(python) export-class <class-name>@endcode

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%requires reflection
# p is the Program of the example above, where PyTest was parsed
p.issueModuleCmd("python", "export-class PyTest");
Class cls = Class::forName(p, "PyTest");
object obj = cls.newObject();
printf("get(): %y\n", obj.get());
    @endcode

    @section python_func_export Exporting Python Functions to Qore

    %Python functions can be explicitly exported to %Qore with the following module command:
    - @code{.py} %module-cmd(python) export-func <func-name>@endcode

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%requires reflection
# p is the Program of the example above, where py_test() was parsed
p.issueModuleCmd("python", "export-func py_test");
Function f = Function::forName(p, "py_test");
# "from python"
printf("py_test(): %y\n", f.call());
    @endcode
*/
